From de79e86e5f182cb7e1bc9c8154f277b03b042e76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 03:59:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/20] fix(ppc64): decode Q4_0/Q4_1/Q5_x/Q4_K/Q6_K with the
 ggml block layout

The VSX quantized kernels did not use the layouts that GGUF files (and
the scalar dequant routines in common/dequant.cpp) use, so every
matmul on these formats produced wrong values on POWER:

- Q4_0/Q4_1 (vec_dot.h, gemv.h): byte j holds element j in its low
  nibble and element j+16 in its high nibble. The kernels interleaved
  the nibbles as if they were elements 2j/2j+1. Q5_0/Q5_1 (gemv.h)
  built their 5-bit values with the same even/odd mapping.
- Q4_K (gemm.h): each 32-byte qs run holds two 32-element sub-blocks
  (low nibbles, then high nibbles); the kernel read 16 bytes per
  sub-block with interleaved nibbles. It also computed the min term as
  -dmin/d times the scale term, which is NaN for an all-zero block.
- Q6_K (gemm.h): the scalar decode did not match the ql/qh/scales
  layout at all.

Fix the nibble mapping for the 32-element formats, and rewrite the
Q4_K/Q6_K block dot products against the dequant layout (vectorized
Q6_K decode, Q8_K bsums for the Q4_K min term, d and dmin applied
separately).

The unit tests had passed because their scalar references and block
generators used the same wrong layouts. Check the quantized kernels
against the scalar dequant routines instead (link dequant.cpp into the
cross-built tests), give the block generators the ggml layout, and add
Q4_K/Q6_K GEMM tests. The old kernels fail the updated tests; the new
ones pass them (run on qemu-ppc with a test-only vec_xl emulation,
since no ppc64le toolchain was available).

The arm64 NEON kernels these were translated from have the same
nibble-interleave bug; they are not changed here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
